## Start the GitLab issue loop

After the repository safeguards are ready, install the runner and enable its
single user-level systemd timer. The loop processes the oldest eligible open,
non-confidential issue without lifecycle or ledger state and never approves or
merges its human-gated merge request.

```bash
uv run agent-work loop run --json
```

Run it from the repository it should operate on. This bare command is a
read-only preview. The runner derives the GitLab host and project from `origin`
and confirms that project through GitLab before reporting eligible work. Use
[`docs/how-to/setup-serial-gitlab-agent-loop.md`](docs/how-to/setup-serial-gitlab-agent-loop.md)
to install the timer. It records redacted lifecycle evidence in the systemd
journal and the XDG-local `agent-work.log`.
Enable linger
and verify it before relying on the timer after logout:

```bash
sudo loginctl enable-linger "$USER"
loginctl show-user "$USER" --property=Linger
```

The second command must report `Linger=yes`. A held checkout lock fails that
pass with temporary-failure exit `75`.

## Serial GitLab agent loop

This project uses GitLab-only polling for one eligible issue at a time. The
runner derives its checkout and GitLab project from the current repository,
then confirms both Git and GitLab access before work begins. A Developer account
can poll open, unprojected issues, so no webhook or inbound service is required:

```bash
uv run agent-work loop run --json
```

An applied run advances one bounded, recoverable state-machine segment. Later
segments publish and verify an MR before stopping at `awaiting-human`; no segment
approves or merges. Enable the user-level
systemd timer only after a human explicitly authorizes publication. For a constrained
identity with effective API access exactly `30` (Developer), the
Developer-readable readiness preflight uses the authoritative effective
default-branch response to require no Developer push, no Developer merge, and
`can_push: false`; it also requires an unambiguously global approval rule with
at least one required approval. When that evidence matches, it skips only the
Maintainer-only `/approvals` audit and recurring Maintainer attestation.
Developer access cannot prove self-approval prevention or per-MR
approval-rule overrides; those checks are intentionally Maintainer-only.
Workflow, project settings, and the runtime never-approve/merge guardrail keep
the agent from approving or merging. This is not a hard same-user security
boundary: inaccessible, absent, or mismatched evidence blocks `--apply`. Git
and `glab` credentials may differ.
See
[`docs/how-to/setup-serial-gitlab-agent-loop.md`](docs/how-to/setup-serial-gitlab-agent-loop.md),
[`docs/reference/serial-gitlab-agent-loop.md`](docs/reference/serial-gitlab-agent-loop.md),
and [`.agents/skills/setup-agent-loop/SKILL.md`](.agents/skills/setup-agent-loop/SKILL.md).
